      "message": "This approach is different from what I\u0027d imagined, but I like yours better. To help myself and outline where we will go from here I\u0027m going to summarize both my envisioned way and yours:\n\nIn my approach, I imagined a Map from pipeline to list of UniformData\u0027s, and as we iterated through the draws initially, we\u0027d add to the pipeline map or append to the pipeline\u0027s list as needed. This would then give us the uniform cache index that would go in the sort key, but it would start at 0 for each pipeline. This isn\u0027t a problem for the sort key since the pipeline is already embedded at higher significance.\n\nAfter iterating all draws, we would iterate the values in the pipeline data map and write all uniform values consecutively for each pipeline. This would mean that each pipeline needs only one BindBufferInfo and the uniform data index stored on the SortKey can be used as the index into the buffer. It makes the loop over sort keys a little simpler, but adds this new intermediate step.\n\nOn the other hand, your approach uses the globally unique uniform data index in the sort key (also valid), and then writes the uniform data as needed while iterating the sort keys. This means that when we change pipelines, the \"index\" used to access the SSBO resets to 0. It can be calculated (in a follow up) based on the byte offset from the binding to where the data was just written. It has a simpler data upload compared to my approach but does slightly more work in the sort key iteration, and (not sure if it\u0027s relevant to GPUs) but puts the data in the same order it\u0027d be accessed.\n\nThe one complicating factor though, is when we encounter a sort key that has already had its uniforms uploaded for a previous draw:\n\n  sort key sequence: [pipeline1, dataA], [pipeline2, dataC], [pipeline1, dataB], [pipeline1, dataA]\n  \nIn my approach we would have two data bindings: [dataA, dataB] and [dataC]. The rough command sequence and SSBO indices we\u0027d send to the gpu would be.\n - bind [dataA, dataB]\n - bind pipeline1\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n - bind [dataC]\n - bind pipeline2\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n - bind [dataA, dataB]\n - bind pipeline1\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d [1, 0]\n \nIn your approach, we would write [dataA] to one binding, then [dataC] to another binding, and then [dataB] to another binding, since we could not concatenate it to the original buffer range we had been using for pipeline1. This means we\u0027d have to either duplicate dataA or rebind between draws like we would for UBOs instead of SSBOs:\n - bind [dataA]\n - bind pipeline1\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n - bind [dataC]\n - bind pipeline2\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n - bind [dataB]\n - bind pipeline1\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n - bind [dataA]\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d 0\n \n or \n - bind [dataB, dataA-copy]\n - bind pipeline1\n - draw with ssbo index \u003d [0, 1]\n \n All of that said, I don\u0027t think it should block this CL, since we\u0027re still in the UBO land, and the issue about indexing already uploaded data only applies to SSBOs. We should just mull over how we want to address it (whether that\u0027s copy, rebind, or organize the uploads more explicitly, or something I haven\u0027t thought of).",
